 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Redundancy:** The initial feed-forward Gaussians are redundant. Without pruning, memory consumption remains high [S1].
-*   **Convergence:** Pretrained priors accelerate training, but fine-tuning must be managed to avoid overfitting to input views if generalization is required [S1].
+*   **Redundancy:** The initial feed-forward Gaussians are redundant, leading to a significant reduction in the number of Gaussians during fine-tuning [S1]. Failure to prune can lead to excessive memory consumption and slower rendering [S1].
+*   **Convergence Speed:** While pretrained priors accelerate convergence, improper initialization can lead to local minima if the initial geometry is significantly misaligned [S1].
 
