 ### ACO-EGR Framework
-ACO-EGR is a unified framework that integrates Adaptive-Scale Color Offset (ACO) and Error-Guided Gaussian Reallocation (EGR) into a multi-scale 3DGS backbone to jointly address scale-dependent color mismatch and primitive-allocation imbalance [S2]. The framework augments a multi-scale 3DGS backbone with two training-stage modules: ACO adapts per-Gaussian color across rendering scales through scale-specific DC color offsets, and EGR refines post-densification Gaussian allocation by moving low-contribution Gaussians to high-error regions within a fixed Gaussian set [S2]. Unlike existing methods that keep fixed color and spatial allocation after densification, ACO-EGR adapts both Gaussian color and primitive allocation together [S2]. It improves rendering quality without modifying the inference-stage rendering process itself; it only changes the training/optimization of the Gaussian parameters [S2].
+ACO-EGR is a unified framework that integrates ACO and EGR into a multi-scale 3DGS backbone to jointly address scale-dependent color mismatch and primitive-allocation imbalance [S2]. The framework augments a multi-scale 3DGS backbone with two training-stage modules: ACO and EGR [S2]. Unlike existing methods that keep fixed color and spatial allocation after densification, ACO-EGR adapts both Gaussian color and primitive allocation together [S2]. ACO-EGR improves rendering quality without modifying the inference-stage rendering process itself; it only changes the training/optimization of the Gaussian parameters [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Accelerated convergence compared to training from scratch due to pretrained priors [S1]. Exact iteration counts depend on the scene and initial quality.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced memory consumption compared to the initial feed-forward state due to pruning of redundant Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods on urban view synthesis tasks [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence, reduced memory footprint, high fidelity [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward initialization [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When efficient training from a pre-initialized state is required, particularly for large-scale or urban scenes where memory is a constraint [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When no pretrained initialization is available or when the initial geometry is severely incorrect [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of Gaussians and training views per iteration, similar to standard 3DGS [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced compared to training from scratch or using unpruned initial estimates, as the number of Gaussians is reduced during fine-tuning [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods, leveraging powerful pretrained weights [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Faster convergence due to pretrained priors; reduced memory consumption; ability to refine initial estimates [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward initialization; may require careful tuning of growing/pruning thresholds to avoid over-pruning [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When a high-quality initial estimate is available (e.g., from a feed-forward model or SfM) and memory/convergence speed are concerns [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When no initial estimate is available, or when the initial estimate is severely misaligned, leading to poor convergence [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in **EvolSplat** (Efficient Volume-based Gaussian Splatting for Urban View Synthesis) [S1]. The method leverages a direct feed-forward process to predict initial 3D Gaussian primitives, which are then optimized using a process similar to vanilla 3DGS but with explicit densification and pruning enabled [S1]. This approach was developed to address the high memory and computational costs associated with training 3DGS from scratch, particularly for large-scale urban environments [S1]. The method was published in 2025 [S1].
+Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced as a method to leverage pretrained priors for efficient scene representation [S1]. It builds on the standard 3DGS training pipeline but adds a final stage of optimization with active densification control [S1]. The method was proposed in the context of EVolSplat, which aims to provide efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting for urban view synthesis [S1].
 
